# The loadout's Supabase connection, read at BUILD TIME by Vite.
#
#   cp web/.env.example web/.env      and fill both in
#
# Vite only exposes variables prefixed `VITE_`, and it INLINES them into the
# bundle when it builds. Two consequences worth knowing before you debug this
# at midnight:
#
#   1. Changing either value needs a restart of `npm run dev:web`, or a
#      rebuild for `npm run build:web`. Nothing re-reads this at runtime.
#   2. Whatever you put here ends up readable in the shipped JavaScript. That
#      is fine for both of these and NOT fine for the secret key — see below.
#
# With neither set, the loadout still runs: it shows "this build has no
# Supabase project set" instead of failing at the first click. That is a real
# state (someone who just cloned the repo), not an error.

# SAME PROJECT as the root .env — the identical URL string. One project, one
# database; the two files differ only in WHICH KEY they carry.
# Supabase dashboard -> Settings -> Data API -> Project URL
# The bare origin only: no /rest/v1, no trailing slash.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=

# The project's PUBLIC key. Supabase dashboard -> Settings -> API Keys.
#
#   sb_publishable_…   <- use this
#   sb_secret_…        <- NOT this. It bypasses row level security and this
#                         file is compiled into the browser bundle.
#
# A legacy `anon` JWT also works if this project predates the new key model;
# Supabase retires that pair at the end of 2026. Either goes in this same
# variable — it was called VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY before the rename, and that
# name is still read as a fallback.
#
# Public is fine here by design: the key identifies the project and nothing
# more, and sql/001_roster.sql is what actually limits a viewer to reading
# their own character.
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
